+# QA report: "You're subscribed" shown, but no email arrives
+
+## Summary
+
+The signup page tells the user they're subscribed **whatever the server answers**. When the API rejects an address (HTTP 422), the page still says *"You're subscribed. Check your inbox for the first issue."* and clears the form. The address was never stored, so no email can arrive.
+
+## Reproduction (real browser, Chromium through Playwright)
+
+1. `node server.js`, then open `http://localhost:3000/`.
+2. Enter `alice+news@example.com` and click **Subscribe**.
+3. The network tab shows `POST /api/subscribe → 422 {"error":"Addresses with + are not supported."}`.
+4. The page shows "You're subscribed. Check your inbox for the first issue." and empties the input.
+
+Observed results:
+
+| Input | API status | API body | Message on the page |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `alice@example.com` | 201 | `{"ok":true}` | You're subscribed… ✅ |
+| `Alice@Example.COM` | 201 | `{"ok":true}` | You're subscribed… ✅ |
+| `bob@mail.example.co.uk` | 201 | `{"ok":true}` | You're subscribed… ✅ |
+| `  carol@example.com  ` | 201 | `{"ok":true}` | You're subscribed… ✅ |
+| `alice+news@example.com` | **422** | `Addresses with + are not supported.` | You're subscribed… ❌ |
+| `a@b.c` | **422** | `Enter a valid email address.` | You're subscribed… ❌ |
+
+The browser's own `type="email"` check accepts both rejected inputs, so the request goes out and the server's validation is the only thing that stops them.
+
+## Root cause
+
+`public/app.js` awaits `fetch()` but never reads the response:
+
+```js
+await fetch('/api/subscribe', { ... });
+status.textContent = "You're subscribed. Check your inbox for the first issue.";
+form.reset();
+```
+
+`fetch` only rejects on network failure. A 4xx/5xx response resolves normally, so the success message runs every time. The `catch` branch ("Network problem") runs only when the network is down. The server's error message (`{ error }`) is never shown.
+
+The users most likely to hit this are people who use `+` addressing (`name+tag@gmail.com`). The server rejects those on purpose (`server.js`, `validate()`), and the UI hides the rejection from them.
+
+## Suggested fix (not applied)
+
+In `app.js`, check `res.ok`. On failure, show `(await res.json()).error` and don't reset the form. I checked this with a patched `app.js` served through Playwright route interception (the repo was not changed). The rejected inputs then show "Addresses with + are not supported." and "Enter a valid email address.", and the valid input still shows the success message.
+
+Separately, product should decide whether rejecting `+` addresses is intended. They are valid and common.
+
+## Regression test
+
+`e2e/subscribe.test.js` (node:test + Playwright). It starts `server.js` on a random port and drives the real page:
+
+- **valid address → success message**: passes.
+- **server rejects `alice+news@example.com` → page must not claim success**: **fails** today.
+- **server rejects `a@b.c` → page must not claim success**: **fails** today.
+
+Run it with:
+
+```
+node --test e2e/subscribe.test.js
+```
+
+Current output:
+
+```
+ok 1 - a valid address is accepted and the user is told so
+not ok 2 - page does not claim success when the server rejects alice+news@example.com
+  error: server answered 422 {"error":"Addresses with + are not supported."} but the page told the user: "You're subscribed. Check your inbox for the first issue."
+not ok 3 - page does not claim success when the server rejects a@b.c
+  error: server answered 422 {"error":"Enter a valid email address."} but the page told the user: "You're subscribed. Check your inbox for the first issue."
+```
+
+The rejection tests pass if the server accepts the address, or if the page doesn't claim success and shows a message. So either a client fix or a decision to allow `+` addresses turns them green.
+
+## Other observations (outside the browser flow, worth confirming)
+
+- **No email is sent from this code at all.** `server.js` only adds the address to an in-memory `Set`. There's no mailer, queue or welcome email. If the "first issue" email is meant to come from somewhere else, that system can't see these subscribers, because:
+- **Subscribers are lost on restart.** The `Set` lives in process memory and nothing persists it.
+- Duplicate signups return 201 again with no indication. That's harmless, but the success message's "check your inbox" may mislead.
